a new proof that the pre-order is well-defined and consistent across the fixpoint.","typicality":0.3,"neighbours":["SRC_005","SRC_006"]},{"content":"Leave the fixpoint untouched and add an interface-level guarantee: for every committed root, emit a short certificate (defender set plus defeat chain) that any external party can verify in polynomial time. Committed-root validity becomes externally auditable without changing the skeptical semantics at all. Criticizable because certificates are themselves artifacts and add storage and serialization cost, and because verifying a certificate does not strengthen the semantics — it only makes the existing validity claim checkable by parties who do not trust the prover.","typicality":0.4,"neighbours":["SRC_003","SRC_001"]},{"content":"Re-scope the commitments: keep determinism and polynomial cost, but demote reinstatement from a derived guarantee to a monitored invariant, with a polynomial-time checker that flags any accepted node whose acceptance cannot be reconstructed from the fixpoint labels; the checker output becomes a new observable. This directly challenges the stated guarantee set and is criticizable for weakening the spec's commitments, and for the checker being able to report violations only after the fact rather than preventing them.","typicality":0.25,"neighbours":["SRC_004","SRC_007"]},{"content":"Reformulate the skeptical fixed point as evaluation of a monotone boolean circuit of depth O(log n) and size O(n^2), preserving determinism and polynomial cost while reducing worst-case depth. This is a complexity-theoretic strengthening that makes the extension cheaper to compute at scale without new semantic artifacts, but is criticizable because the skeptical semantics' non-monotone defeat structure may not admit such a shallow monotone circuit, and the claimed size bound may fail for dense attack graphs.","typicality":0.5,"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_003"]}]}